## NFR-003: Scalability

**Throughput model:**

| Practice size | Patients | Documents/day (baseline) |
|---------------|----------|--------------------------|
| Smaller practice | 6,000 | 250 |
| PCN Hub | 100,000 | 1,300 |

**Scaling ratio:** ~0.0417 documents per patient per day (250 / 6,000). Linear scale applies as patient list size increases between these bounds and beyond.

**Important population note:** Elderly patient populations generate significantly more inbound documents than younger populations. Practices with older demographic profiles should be expected to operate at the upper end of the scaling range even at lower total patient list sizes. The system must accommodate demographic-driven throughput variation without performance degradation.

**Acceptance Criteria:**
- [ ] System handles the documented per-practice throughput at the performance targets defined in NFR-001 without degradation.
- [ ] System scales linearly as patient list size increases beyond 100,000 patients (e.g. large PCN or federated practice group).
- [ ] Peak load handling: system must sustain 3× baseline throughput for periods of up to 1 hour (e.g. morning document burst, post-weekend backlog).
- [ ] Concurrent users: system must support at least 10 concurrent active users per practice without performance degradation.
- [ ] Scalability is achieved without manual infrastructure intervention (auto-scaling required).
- [ ] Throughput targets may be refined following pilot data; current figures are working assumptions based on stated practice sizes.

**Scaling formula (working assumption):**
> `documents_per_day = (patient_list_size / 6,000) × 250`
>
> This may be adjusted upward for practices with above-average elderly populations.

